ng)" 13194139535490 17592186045443 true] [17592186389181 1071 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" 13194139535490 17592186045443 false] [17592186389181 1071 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" 13194139535490 17592186045443 true] [17592186389181 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)" 13194139535490 17592186045443 false] [17592186389181 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\fr